Z||000000||LT1 Chop Top||03-31-2001||06:06 PM||||All,<P>What grade of oil should be used in an 88 GT?  10w-30 or 5w-30 and why??<BR>It says 5w on the oil fill cap, but isn't 5w for very cold conditions?<P>Thanks||||reg||1
Z||000001||rogergarrison||03-31-2001||08:41 PM||||I use 5w50 synthetic all year round in ohio. my car is an almost daily driver.<P>------------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=1234884&a=9116379&p=34556781"> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||Pontiaddict||03-31-2001||09:26 PM||||The car manufacturers found out that by using thinner oil and runner tighter tolerances in the motor, fuel efficiency and the longevity of their motors improved. ||||reg||1
Z||000003||bubbajoexx||03-31-2001||11:15 PM||||in temp  above 75 deg. use 10w40 as the manufacturer recomends in the owners manual and in winter 1ow30||||reg||1
Z||000004||88formula||04-01-2001||01:38 AM||||Why choose 10w-30w when you can use 5w-30w instead? They both act as a 30 weight when fully warmed up but the 5w-30w multi-viscosity will flow more freely when the temperature gets low protecting you engine parts better. The oil will get to the engine parts quicker with the 5w-30w in cold temperatures.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/88formula_logo_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000005||bubbajoexx||04-01-2001||03:09 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by 88formula:<BR><B>Why choose 10w-30w when you can use 5w-30w instead? They both act as a 30 weight when fully warmed up but the 5w-30w multi-viscosity will flow more freely when the temperature gets low protecting you engine parts better. The oil will get to the engine parts quicker with the 5w-30w in cold temperatures.<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I guess you do not know the meaning of multi grade 5w30 means at 0 deg. the oil gets no thicker than #30 weight at running temp the oil dose not get thiner than #5 weight and #5 oil is as thin as hydrolic fluid do you want that in your engine not me ||||reg||9
Z||000006||rogergarrison||04-01-2001||03:54 PM||||i used the 5w50 in everything for years. my mercedes had 275,000, lincoln had 300,000, fiero now has 80,000 no engine failures or probs. ||||reg||1
Z||000007||saleenfiero||04-01-2001||05:19 PM||||I use synthetic 5W50 in mine, does anybody make mineral 5W50?<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/fiero1_12.jpg"> <P>------------------<BR>'86 SE - My car is actually a MacLaren F1 with a Fiero body kit; or not.||||reg||8
Z||000008||88formula||04-01-2001||06:56 PM||||bubbajoexx the reason why engine manufacturers use multi viscosity oil is for better protection during cold start up without loosing the protection of the heavy weight oil when the engine is warmed up and it improves fuel economy. When the oil is cold 5w-30w multi viscosity oil has the viscosity of a regular 5 weight oil but when the oil is hot it has the viscosity of a 30 weight oil. <P>The only difference between 5w-30w and 10w-30w oil is that the 5w-30w oil has a greater viscosity rating and flows better in cold temperatures than the latter so why use 10w-30w oil? They both have the same hot oil viscosity ratings. Most engine bearing wear occurs in the few seconds during start-up that it takes for the oil pump to pump the oil to the engine bearings. With 5w-30w oil it takes less time.<P>This website has some great information about oil. <P>  <A HREF="http://www.vtr.org/maintain/oil-overview.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.vtr.org/maintain/oil-overview.html</A> <P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/88formula_logo_1.JPG">  <p>[This message has been edited by 88formula (edited 04-01-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000009||KRMFiero||04-01-2001||09:39 PM||||i run 20W-50 synthetic in my duke (and some mistery oil) works good, or seems to...       is this oil to thick for the duke though? i get great oil pressure, with 10W-30 i had shitty oil pressure<P>Kyle||||reg||1
Z||000010||HAGS||04-02-2001||02:16 AM||||I heard that 10w-30 is better than 5w-30 unless you really need the 5 in freezing weather...I cant think of a way to explain the reasoning right now but the reason I heard was pretty good.<P>Also I usually take off right away with a cold(doesnt get cold in Texas) engine...I'd rather have 10 grade protecting my parts rather than being too thin with 5 grade.||||reg||1
Z||000011||Predator||04-02-2001||07:43 AM||||Since I live in Georgia, and run a Turbo on my V6 it was recommended that I run 10W 40W synthetic blend oil. Seems to work fine for me here. <P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Fiero4_1.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000012||SteveJ||04-02-2001||09:00 AM||||The move to 5 weight oil was prompted by the need for better gas milage by the manufacturers. With the government breathing down their neck they squeezed as much mileage as they could by using thinner oil.<P>Manufacturers don't care how long the engine lasts as long as they all make it through the warantee period. Other than that they don't want a reputation for bad engines. Their goal here is to make 'em just good enough.<P>The 5 refers to the length of the polimers in the base oil being used before additives come into the picture. 10's are longer than 5's and give better protection. But going too long can be a problem too.<P>I run 10w-30 despite the recommendation for 5w-30 on the car.<P>( Frankly guys as a child I remember cars specifying straight 30 weight oil, and non-detergent at that. )||||reg||1
Z||000013||Formula88||04-02-2001||04:06 PM||||5W-30 is also probably best, overall, for a new engine.  If you have high miles, your clearances are getting a bit looser, you may benefit from 10w-30. I wouldn't consider running 20W-50 unless it was extreme summer heat, or the car has mods that generate more heat.  Watch how fast your oil pressure comes up when you start your car.  Until it gets up to pressure, you've got parts running without oil.  The 5w-30 will be quicker to start flowing due to it's better cold performance.  ||||reg||3
Z||000014||rogergarrison||04-02-2001||06:20 PM||||[QUOTE]Originally posted by saleenfiero:<BR>[B]I use synthetic 5W50 in mine, does anybody make mineral 5W50?<BR>  <P>Aeroshell (general aviation oil) is mineral,you could buy it at an airport<P>||||reg||1
